To solve this problem, you need to implement a **Global Tooltip Provider**.

Since Next.js components are isolated, you cannot rely on `useState` inside a single component to track the state of tooltips opened elsewhere. Instead, you must lift the state up to a Context Provider that wraps your entire application (or the specific layout where you want the tooltip to work).

Here is a complete, copy-pasteable solution using **React Context** and **Tailwind CSS** (standard in Next.js).

### 1. Create the Context and Provider
First, create a file to hold the state logic. This will be the "brain" that knows which tooltip is currently open.

`context/TooltipContext.tsx`

```tsx
'use client'; // Required for Next.js App Router

import React, { createContext, useContext, useState, useCallback, ReactNode } from 'react';

// Define the shape of our data
interface TooltipContextType {
  openTooltip: (id: string) => void;
  closeTooltip: (id: string) => void;
  isOpen: (id: string) => boolean;
}

const TooltipContext = createContext<TooltipContextType | undefined>(undefined);

export const TooltipProvider = ({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) => {
  // We use a Map to store multiple open tooltips if needed, 
  // or a simple string if only one is allowed at a time.
  // For this example, we assume only one tooltip is open at a time.
  const [activeTooltipId, setActiveTooltipId] = useState<string | null>(null);

  const openTooltip = useCallback((id: string) => {
    setActiveTooltipId(id);
  }, []);

  const closeTooltip = useCallback((id: string) => {
    // If we want to allow multiple tooltips, we would filter the array here.
    // If only one is allowed, we just set to null.
    setActiveTooltipId(null); 
  }, []);

  const isOpen = useCallback((id: string) => {
    return activeTooltipId === id;
  }, [activeTooltipId]);

  return (
    <TooltipContext.Provider value={{ openTooltip, closeTooltip, isOpen }}>
      {children}
    </TooltipContext.Provider>
  );
};

export const useTooltip = () => {
  const context = useContext(TooltipContext);
  if (!context) {
    throw new Error('useTooltip must be used within a TooltipProvider');
  }
  return context;
};
```

### 2. Wrap your Application
You must wrap your app so the context is available globally. In Next.js App Router, this usually goes in `layout.tsx`.

`app/layout.tsx`

```tsx
import type { Metadata } from "next";
import { TooltipProvider } from "@/context/TooltipContext"; // Adjust path
import "./globals.css";

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: "Next.js Tooltip",
};

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: Readonly<{
  children: React.ReactNode;
}>) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        {/* This wraps the entire app */}
        <TooltipProvider>
          {children}
        </TooltipProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

