### Approach 1: Static Generation (Recommended)
This is the most common use case. You have a JSON file (e.g., `schema.json`), and you want to generate a Dart class (e.g., `model.dart`) that represents that data.

#### Step 1: Install the JSON Parser
You will need the `json` package (usually included in `dart:json` or `json_serializable`).

#### Step 2: Create the Generator Script
Create a file named `generate_models.dart`. This script will read your JSON and write the Dart code.

```dart
import 'dart:io';
import 'dart:convert';

void main() {
  // 1. Read the JSON file
  final jsonFile = File('data.json');
  if (!jsonFile.existsSync()) {
    print('data.json not found!');
    return;
  }

  final jsonString = jsonFile.readAsStringSync();
  final Map<String, dynamic> data = json.decode(jsonString);

  // 2. Define the output file path
  final outputFile = File('generated_model.dart');

  // 3. Build the Dart code string
  final dartCode = '''
import 'package:json_annotation/json_annotation.dart';

class ${data['name']} {
  ${data['name']}.({
    ${_generateFields(data['properties'])}
  });

  ${data['name']}.copyWith({
    ${_generateFields(data['properties'])}
  });

  @override
  String toString() {
    return '${data['name']}(${_generateToString(data['properties'])})';
  }
}

String _generateFields(Map<String, dynamic> props) {
  return '${props.keys.map((k) => '  ${k}: this.${k},').join(',\\n')}';
}

String _generateToString(Map<String, dynamic> props) {
  return '${props.keys.map((k) => '${k}: ${k}').join(', ')}';
}
''';

  // 4. Write the code to the file
  outputFile.writeAsStringSync(dartCode);
  print('Generated ${outputFile.path} successfully!');
}
