In Java, both overloading and overriding are important concepts related to method definitions. They are often confused with each other, but they serve different purposes and have distinct characteristics.

Overloading

Overloading is the process of defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameters. The methods can have different data types, different numbers of parameters, or different combinations of data types and numbers of parameters. The compiler determines which method to call based on the arguments passed to the method.

Example of Overloading:

